  dailyRead: "Start with the print, because for once the day's scheduled event landed before this file was written. UK August CPI came in at 3.1% year on year against a 3.1% forecast and 2.9% previously, with the monthly rate at 0.5% from 0.3%. That is exactly the number yesterday's file said would preserve the score and the vote - and it did - but the composition is more interesting than the headline. Core inflation held at 2.6% against a 2.7% forecast, so it did not accelerate at all. Put the two together and you have a headline driven by energy passing through into a core that is going nowhere, which is precisely the split the Monetary Policy Committee has been arguing about since July. The three hawks - Pill, Greene and Mann - get a 3.1% headline at a fifteen-month high to point at. The six holders get a core that missed and a claimant count that blew out to +27.8k yesterday against an +8.3k forecast. Nothing in this morning's data moves a single vote, which is why the consensus is still 3-0-6 for a hold at 3.75% tomorrow at 11:00Z, and why GBP stays at 0. What sterling did do is outperform: it is the best G10 currency on the day at +0.09% and still the best on the week at -0.42% against the dollar, GBP/USD 1.34899. A currency that takes a broken labour print on Tuesday and an unaccelerating core on Wednesday and still leads the board is a currency with a bid under it, and that is the single reason GBP/USD SHORT is cut to LOW-MED and EUR/GBP LONG with it. Second, the dollar's streak is over, and this file should say so plainly because it spent two days building the case for it. DXY is 99.569, down 0.07%, and it lost ground to five of seven peers today: GBP +0.09%, EUR +0.05%, CHF +0.05%, AUD +0.01%, against CAD -0.03%, JPY -0.04% and NZD -0.07%. The weekly board is untouched - the dollar is still up 0.76% and still higher against all seven over five sessions - so this is a pause, not a turn, and USD holds +2. It is emphatically not promoted to +3, for the same reason as yesterday and now with more force: DXY is minus 0.07% on the month while carrying an 86-to-90% probability of a hike to 4.00%, which says the move is fully bought and nothing beyond it is priced. That question resolves at 18:00Z tonight and there is no way to pre-position for it, which is the organising fact of this entire file. Third, and this is the day's genuine change of view, the haven failure has stopped. Gold is 4329.73, up 0.87%; silver is 64.584, up 1.48%; and they did that while WTI fell 0.99% to 104.779. Every prior session of this run had the opposite sign - bullion down, oil up, insurance being paid in energy rather than metal. One session does not overturn a month, and the weekly numbers still favour the short: gold minus 1.56%, silver minus 3.99%. But the reason to act is not the bounce, it is what the bounce is made of. Managed-money positioning in silver is crowded long at roughly 228,000 net contracts into a binary event, and a crowded long that starts rising the morning of the event is the market hedging, not capitulating. Holding a directional metals short through a dot plot that could move real rates either way, against that positioning, is a bad risk for a 4H book. So XAU/USD and XAG/USD are STOOD DOWN from SHORT to RANGE. That is a de-risking into an event, not a reversal: the real-rates thesis is intact and the shorts come back if the dots are lifted. Fourth, copper is stood down as well, and here the reasoning is structural rather than tactical. XCU is 6.4118, up 0.68% today but down 5.76% on the week, and the week's collapse now has an identified cause - reports that the United States is reconsidering the refined-metal import tariffs that had been pulling hundreds of thousands of tonnes across the Atlantic, which knocked futures almost 5% in a session. That matters because the tariff-arbitrage trade was the short's whole engine. Once the arbitrage unwinds, what is left is the physical market, and the physical market is tight: LME live stocks have been drained to roughly 90,000 tonnes, global mine output contracted 1.1% in the first half with Chile down 6.6%, Indonesian concentrate down 32% and DRC down 34%. A positioning short whose catalyst has already been paid, standing in front of a 90,000-tonne inventory, is a short that snaps - yesterday's file said so in terms, and today it acts on its own warning. XCU/USD goes to RANGE. Fifth, oil. WTI fell 0.99% today after rising 9.09% on the week and 24.00% on the month, and Brent is 108.126, down 0.57%. Nothing in the supply picture has changed: Saudi Arabia's East-West pipeline - the route that bypasses Hormuz - is still shut with no restart timeline from Aramco, Houthi attacks on Saudi Arabia resumed this week, Libya's national oil company has suspended two oilfields and a pumping station amid protests, and the US and Iran are exchanging tanker-for-tanker strikes around the strait under a policy Washington approved this month. A one-percent down day inside that is profit-taking after a 24% month, not a resolution. USOIL LONG stays, and it is now the ONLY high-conviction trade in the file, precisely because it is the one position whose thesis does not depend on a central bank speaking in the next three days. Which brings the book to its organising idea. The board is USD +2; EUR, AUD, CAD and JPY +1; GBP and CHF 0; NZD -1. Almost every gap on it has an event attached inside sixty-five hours - a dollar leg resolves at 18:00Z, a kiwi leg at 22:45Z, a sterling leg tomorrow at 11:00Z and a yen leg on Friday at 02:30Z. The trades that survive that filter are the CHF crosses, because the SNB does not meet until September 24 and is universally expected to sit at 0.00%: EUR/CHF, AUD/CHF and CAD/CHF LONG are event-free expressions of the same view the dollar book expresses with event risk, and CAD/CHF is upgraded to MEDIUM on that basis alone. On the currencies themselves: the euro holds +1 on a genuinely hawkish central bank - a unanimous hike to a 2.50% deposit rate on September 10, the 2027 inflation projection lifted to 2.5%, 2026 growth upgraded to 0.9% - against an energy bill that keeps worsening, with EU gas at 82.69 up 3.29% today, 33.89% on the month and 155% on the year. Australia holds +1 with the top carry on the board at 4.35% and a copper bounce beneath it. Canada holds +1 on energy terms of trade alone, and it slips below Australia within the tier today because oil fell and copper rose - its domestic data is still contracting outright with employment minus 41.7k, services PMI 46.8 and composite 47.8, and the Bank of Canada is frozen at 2.25% until October 28. Japan holds +1: wage growth 4.7%, CPI 1.9%, core 1.8%, unemployment 2.4%, and a hike to a 31-year high on Friday that 97% of economists expect - but speculators have flipped net long yen for the first time since February, which is the definition of a crowded trade into a priced event, so the yen crosses stay RANGE rather than short. Switzerland stays at 0 on the same contradiction as yesterday: the best data on the board - GDP up 1.5% on the quarter and 2.3% on the year, manufacturing PMI 57.1, unemployment 3.0% - attached to a 0% policy rate and a central bank that has signalled it will intervene against appreciation. Good economy, unownable currency, ideal funding leg. And New Zealand stays at minus 1 and worst on the board, down 0.07% today, 1.49% on the week and 2.52% on the month at 0.57531, with Q2 GDP tonight at 22:45Z forecast at +0.1% against +0.8% previously. The warning on that one is specific: ASB expects 0.3%, above both the consensus and the RBNZ's own flat estimate, so the risk on the kiwi short is an upside surprise arriving five hours after the Fed. That is why NZD/USD comes down from HIGH to MEDIUM - not because the view changed, but because two binary events now sit inside one session and the position cannot be managed through both.",

