  geopolitics: "The oil shock is now seven months old and it is still the transmission mechanism through which everything else on this board is priced. Iran effectively closed the Strait of Hormuz - roughly 20% of global seaborne oil - in retaliation for the US-Israeli strikes that began on February 28, and the conflict has since migrated almost entirely to sea. In the last fortnight the United States has adopted an explicit tanker-for-tanker policy, striking three Iranian tankers in a single weekend while the IRGC claims strikes on three tankers and three US-linked vessels and has fired a ballistic missile toward US warships near the strait. Both sides are now contesting control of the waterway directly rather than through proxies, which is a materially harder situation to de-escalate than the proxy phase was. Layered on top of that are three separate supply interruptions that have nothing to do with Iranian action: Saudi Arabia's East-West pipeline - the very route built to bypass Hormuz - has been shut since a drone attack on September 11 with no restart timeline from Aramco, Houthi strikes on Saudi Arabia resumed this week, and Libya's national oil company has suspended two oilfields and a pumping station amid protests. That is why WTI is 104.779 and up 24.00% on the month, and why today's 0.99% pullback should be read as profit-taking rather than as the start of a resolution - nothing in the physical picture changed today. The FX transmission runs four ways and today it produced an unusual configuration. First, it is a terms-of-trade shock, and it sorts the board accordingly: Canada is the only G10 economy paid by it, which is the whole CAD case and the reason Canada beats the commodity bloc despite a domestic economy with two PMIs under 50, while the euro area is the most directly taxed, with EU gas at 82.69 up 33.89% on the month and about 155% on the year, feeding an August HICP of 3.3% with energy inflation at 14.3% while core fell to 2.4%. New Zealand sits at the far end of that same axis: its 4.1% headline inflation is explicitly imported fuel cost, which is why the RBNZ hiked on September 2 and then stopped. Second, it is an inflation shock that central banks are having to decide whether to look through, and they are splitting on it - the Fed hikes into it tonight, the ECB hiked into it on September 10, the BoE has three of nine members wanting to, and the RBNZ hiked once and then paused. Third, the haven channel, which is where today differed. For the entire run to date bullion had failed to respond, because the insurance premium was being paid in crude rather than metal and because real rates were rising underneath it. Today gold rose 0.87% and silver 1.48% while crude fell - the first inversion of that pattern - which reads as pre-event hedging rather than a change in the geopolitical assessment, but it is enough to stand the metal shorts down into the dot plot. Fourth, the franc. Switzerland is the classic recipient of a Middle East haven bid and it has not been allowed to receive one, because the SNB has signalled an explicit willingness to intervene against appreciation, with sight deposits rising ahead of the September 24 meeting. That is what converts the best fundamental data on this board into the cleanest funding currency on it. The tail risk to watch is unchanged in shape and slightly sharper in degree: a genuine closure of Hormuz rather than the current contested passage would take crude sharply higher and force every currency here to be re-sorted by energy balance rather than by policy rate, while a credible ceasefire or an Aramco restart would unwind the USOIL LONG, the CAD case and the NZD short simultaneously.",

